{"schema": 1, "kind": "review", "id": "rev-20261123T132423Z-278552aa", "ts": "2026-11-23T13:30:16Z", "actor": {"worktree": "prawduct", "plugin": "3.9.1-dev", "branch": "fix/chunk-plan-order", "build": "95308783037f"}, "body": {"base_commit": "a04758d4cb3c98e1d0f941ea9a144d35a76d9d05", "base_tree": "9779670b21c2f1a797b5cb948684edcb3bd99a8c", "head_tree": "aa7399bdf5c95859326984dc4182f9fde9d0c2e9", "head_commit": "32a0ff594903a41581630a9955e6a9428eafb66a", "dispatch_commit": "32a0ff594903a41581630a9955e6a9428eafb66a", "mode": "cumulative (bundle review, ready for merge)", "mode_chosen_by": "explicit-args", "stage": "boundary", "tier": "escalate", "roster": [{"role": "correctness", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "design", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}, {"role": "sustainability", "model": "claude-opus-5-5"}], "files_reviewed": ["plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/backlog/upstream.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/methodology/delegation.md", "plugin/methodology/planning.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/skills/report-bug/SKILL.md", "tests/test_backlog_upstream.py", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_evidence_store.py", "tests/test_plan_hook_cmd.py", "tests/test_plan_retract.py", "tests/test_plan_status.py", "tests/test_review_interval_extension.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_changed": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-chunk-plan-order.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/chunk-plan-order.md", ".prawduct/changes/delegate-ceiling-whole-tree-guards.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook", "plugin/lib/backlog/upstream.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "plugin/lib/evidence.py", "plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/methodology/delegation.md", "plugin/methodology/planning.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/skills/report-bug/SKILL.md", "tests/test_backlog_upstream.py", "tests/test_critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_evidence_store.py", "tests/test_plan_hook_cmd.py", "tests/test_plan_retract.py", "tests/test_plan_status.py", "tests/test_review_interval_extension.py", "tests/test_reviewer_payload_budget.py", "tests/test_v5_methodology.py"], "files_oracle": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-chunk-plan-order.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/chunk-plan-order.md", ".prawduct/changes/delegate-ceiling-whole-tree-guards.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md"], "findings": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "A chunk named by its scoped (Chunk N) subject gets route 'proposal', so the D8 deferral and the D6 flag question the builder's own words", "recommendation": "Scope: class. Why it broke: `_route` (plugin/lib/plan_status.py) gives one kind, `proposal`, to a chunk a path proposed (the code's naming) and to one a scoped `(Chunk N)` commit subject proposed in a final/cumulative. Design D2 lists the second as one of the builder's words ('Words are `--chunks`, a scoped subject in a whole-plan proposal, and a span'), and Chunk 07's revised rule says only the code's own naming is questioned. Both readers that branch on the kind treat a subject-named chunk as the code's naming. (1) `critic_mode._done_chunks_touched_clause` excludes only `declared` and `span`, and it excludes the reviewed chunks only when the newest review named exactly one. So after a multi-chunk boundary `cumulative` on a short plan, where every chunk is named by its scoped subject, any fix touching a chunk's Deliverables gets 'if that was wrong, `prawduct-hook plan retract <N> --all`' appended to the OWED_BOUNDARY deferral. That is the false alarm Chunk 07 dogfooded and fixed for `--chunks`, now on the ordinary fix-after-boundary-review path. (2) `ahead_of_line` gives a delegated chunk landed by its scoped subject the unqualified 'if chunk N's work is not built, retract' wording. D2 calls exactly that shape sanctioned, so it should get 'expected when chunks are built out of order'. No test covers the cumulative case: test_review_interval_extension.py's D8 tests all dispatch `chunk` mode. Closure: derive a distinct route kind for a subject-named chunk from the fact alone. `chunks_evidence[c].commits` is non-empty only for a chunk in `kept_by_words` (chunk_proposal fills commits only `if c in by_words`), so no git walk is needed. Then have both readers treat that kind as words, and pin it with a test of a multi-chunk cumulative followed by a fix. Members: `ahead_of_line` and `_done_chunks_touched_clause`, the only readers of `ChunkState.route`. The owner confirmed the Chunk 07 rule 'as written' on 2026-11-23, and its parenthetical files every proposal under the code's naming. If that classification of subject-named chunks is intended, record it as a ruling beside that confirmation rather than leave the parenthetical and D2 in conflict.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "tests/test_review_interval_extension.py"], "fid": "R-1"}, {"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "severity": "warning", "title": "Chunk 07's span exclusion from the D8 deferral clause has no test", "recommendation": "Scope: instance. Commit 80cf8ba7 added `and getattr(progress.states.get(c), 'route', None) not in ('declared', 'span')` to `_done_chunks_touched_clause`, and its message claims 'a span's scoped subject' is now never questioned. Only the `declared` member is pinned: test_a_later_chunk_editing_a_chunk_the_builder_named_says_nothing and test_a_chunk_the_builder_named_is_not_questioned both use `--chunks`. Dropping 'span' from the tuple leaves the suite green. The only span-route test, test_critic_consolidate.py::test_a_span_ahead_of_an_open_chunk_is_flagged_as_expected, asserts `ahead_of_line` and not the deferral. tests.md: 'A CLASS finding closed where it was NOTICED isn't closed \u2014 fix every site ... AND make a test fail for each'. Add a deferral test in which a done-by-span chunk's Deliverables are touched by later work and the message carries no 'plan retract', and see it red with 'span' removed.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_mode.py", "tests/test_review_interval_extension.py"], "fid": "R-2"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "severity": "warning", "title": "The exit-8 refusal labels the earliest proposed chunk as 'the chunk you built', and the skill row tells the agent to copy that line", "recommendation": "Scope: instance (one renderer, one skill row). Why it broke: the remedy was written for the in-order case and never checked against the case the refusal exists for. `_chunks_ambiguous_reason` prints `re-run with --chunks <proposal.chunks[0]> for the chunk you built`, and SKILL.md's exit-8 row says 're-dispatch with stdout's --chunks line for the work you built; never pass both'. `begin_review`'s own comment gives the reason it refuses rather than narrows: narrowing to the earliest chunk names the wrong one when the builder works out of order and touches an earlier chunk's path in passing. Take that case: 01 and 02 done, 04 built ahead, a 03 file edited in passing. The proposal is 03,04. The line offered as 'the chunk you built' is `--chunks 03`, which certifies the unbuilt 03 (the #1141 class this work closes). The only other line passes both, which the skill row forbids. No line names 04. An agent following the row as written can only certify the wrong chunk or break the row. Fix: render one `--chunks <id>` re-run line per proposed chunk (the paths are already listed beside each), keep the all-of-them line, and word the row as 'the line naming the chunk you built'. Pin it with a test that every proposed id gets its own re-run line. Design D3 prescribed '--chunks <earliest>', so record the change under the plan's Decisions made mid-build ('method is advice'). Per the authoring rule 'A REMEDY named in a checklist must be tested against the predicate that produced the condition'.", "files": ["plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md"], "fid": "R-3"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "severity": "warning", "title": "The classification of a route as the builder's own words lives in two modules as string literals, and the two copies already drifted once", "recommendation": "Scope: instance (two members: `plan_status.ahead_of_line`, line ~1070, `st.route in (\"declared\", \"span\")`; and `critic_mode._done_chunks_touched_clause`, line ~1975, `getattr(progress.states.get(c), \"route\", None) not in (\"declared\", \"span\")`). Why it broke: the route kind is a bare string with no owner for its meaning, so each reader re-decides which kinds are the builder's words. That decision is the one Chunk 07 had to fix: the deferral questioned chunk 01, which `--chunks 01` had named, while the table's wording already treated it as the builder's. Whoever next adds a route kind, or changes which kinds count, updates one site. Then the Stop deferral and the `plan status` flag disagree about the same row. Fix: give `plan_status` the one answer, for example a `ChunkState.named_by_builder` property or a module constant beside `_route`, and type `route` as a `Literal` (the module already imports it). Both readers call it, and the `getattr` on a typed dataclass goes away. The plan's architecture disposition ('every fact has one home \u2026 D3, D4, D6 and D8 read its answer rather than recompute it') holds for plan order but not for this classification.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/critic_mode.py"], "fid": "R-4"}, {"goal": "Records Pass", "severity": "warning", "title": "owner-rulings 2026-11-23 item 3 sequences develop's two unpushed commits as a later PR, but this branch carries both", "recommendation": "Instance. Why it broke: fix/chunk-plan-order was cut from local develop after a04758d4 and 792d8c06 landed there unpushed, so the branch's merge-base with origin/develop is 4b37f25f, and a PR from it to develop carries 12 commits, including both delegate-ceiling commits. (792d8c06 is inside this bundle; a04758d4 is the cumulative's base_commit, so this review did not cover its diff, yet it ships in the PR.) Item 3 says those commits 'ship as a PR after fix/chunk-plan-order's'. Following it at `/prawduct:pr`, a builder opens a PR that silently contains the delegate-ceiling work under a chunk-plan-order description, then opens a second PR with nothing in it. Before `/prawduct:pr`, decide with the owner: either push or PR develop's two commits first (which reverses item 3's order), or let them ride this PR. Record the answer as a dated entry beneath item 3, not as an edit to the ruling text, and name the extra commits in the PR body.", "files": [".prawduct/owner-rulings.md"], "fid": "R-5"}], "counts": {"blocking": 0, "warning": 5, "note": 0}, "observations": [{"goal": "Nothing Is Broken", "title": "A verify-resolutions fact records chunks_declared false with empty chunks_evidence, so where it is the certifying fact its route reads as an unrecorded proposal", "recommendation": "`_dispatch_proposal` returns `ChunkProposal(list)` for verify-resolutions, with no evidence, and `begin_review` records `chunks_declared: false`. This branch's own store shows it: rev-20261123T011411Z-f141f3fa and rev-20261123T013214Z-724be6a9. `_route` would render such a fact as 'named by its proposal; what proposed it is not recorded' and give it the questioned `proposal` kind, although the chunk was inherited from the chain. In practice the chained chunk review certifies first, because `still_open` makes the earliest certifying fact win once its blocker resolves, so this surfaces only when that original fact cannot certify. Note it, or have `_route` read an inherited (verify-resolutions) fact's route from its chain, if that case is ever met.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py"], "oid": "O-1"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "Two proposal wrappers no production code calls any more, and one stub that now stubs nothing", "recommendation": "`plan_status.propose_chunks` lost its only plugin caller when `extension_deferral_owed` switched to `chunk_proposal(...).touched`, which design D4 anticipated. `critic_consolidate._proposed_chunks` lost its only caller when `begin_review` switched to `_dispatch_proposal`. Only tests call them now. `tests/test_review_interval_extension.py:654` still monkeypatches `plan_status.propose_chunks` in `_owed`, which no longer reaches it; the test holds through the `_paths_since_tree` stub instead. Consider pointing the tests at `chunk_proposal` / `_dispatch_proposal` and deleting the wrappers and the stub. No behaviour depends on it.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/lib/critic_consolidate.py", "tests/test_review_interval_extension.py"], "oid": "O-2"}, {"goal": "The Design Is Sound", "title": "retraction_plan returns a list or a single target, and the single form admits a status its caller misreports", "recommendation": "`retraction_plan` returns `list[RetractionTarget] | RetractionTarget` and ends with `return first` when nothing was planned. `first` can carry status `ok` or `not-certified`, and `_cmd_plan_retract --all` reports any non-`already` single target as a refusal named by that status. I found no path that reaches `ok` with an empty plan. Still, a small result type (pairs plus an optional refusal/already note) would make that case unrepresentable rather than unlikely. Borderline: one caller.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py", "plugin/bin/prawduct-hook"], "oid": "O-3"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "building.md's How: parenthetical can be read as adding the whole-tree guards only when there is no Delegate verification row", "recommendation": "'(the `Delegate verification` row, else the narrowest run covering its own change, plus the whole-tree guards; \u2026)' parses either way. delegation.md, which the line sends the reader to, settles it with 'Either way', so this is unlikely to mislead. A wording like 'the row, else the narrowest run \u2026, and either way the whole-tree guards' would carry the same meaning on its own.", "files": ["plugin/methodology/building.md"], "oid": "O-4"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Small description gaps in plan_status", "recommendation": "The module docstring's function index does not list the new public `done_chunks_touched` or `ahead_of_line`. `ahead_of_line`'s two wordings differ in small ways ('if 04's work' against 'if chunk 04's work'). Neither is read by anything. No edit is wanted for its own sake.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "oid": "O-5"}, {"goal": "Everything Is Coherent", "title": "Framework-Specific Checks: Generality passes, Pipeline Coverage does not apply", "recommendation": "The report-bug chunk-state paragraph, the planning.md plan-order and ship-in-parts text, and the delegation whole-tree-guard class are stated at the level of a property or of prawduct's own components, so they serve any onboarded product. No new artifact template, discovery dimension or Critic check was added, so Pipeline Coverage does not apply. SKILL.md step 4 is still one very long paragraph, but that predates this work (Cumulative Health, observation).", "oid": "O-6"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "The plan still marks the two owner-confirmed design calls as inferred", "recommendation": "build-plan-chunk-plan-order.md Goals still reads 'Inferred (not in the design)' for D3's guard-refusal recording, and 'Not settled by the design; inferred' for D8's exclusion rule. owner-rulings.md 2026-11-23 item 4 confirms both, but the plan does not point to that confirmation. A later reader of the plan sees two open inferences, which Goal 5 rates WARNING. A clause per entry, 'confirmed 2026-11-23 (owner-rulings.md)', would close it. Authoring rule: 'A ruling recorded only where the deciders read it is NOT recorded'. This is not worth a round on its own; fold it into whatever commit the PR boundary needs anyway.", "files": [".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-chunk-plan-order.md"], "oid": "O-7"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "The dev build label sits inside the digested and keyed report body, and no decision records that", "recommendation": "plugin_build() feeds version_label() inside render_report(), and both payload_digest (the --approve token) and source_key (the idempotency key) hash that body. On a -dev checkout, the approval token and the dedup key therefore change whenever the plugin tree's commit or its +dirty state changes between preview and send. The result is an approval-mismatch, which the render_preview docstring says 'reads to the operator as their own mistake'. The Chunk 05 decision records the reader choice (read_plugin_build) and the fact that the marker's found_in stays bare, but not this interaction. Record it as accepted, or keep the build out of the digested bytes.", "files": ["plugin/lib/backlog/upstream.py"], "oid": "O-8"}, {"goal": "Decisions Were Deliberate", "title": "ChunkProposal.withheld is read only by tests", "recommendation": "chunk_proposal builds its withheld-chunk notes from _PathAnswer.withheld. No production reader consumes the public ChunkProposal.withheld tuple: grep finds only tests/test_plan_status.py. The plan's Chunk 01 deliverables list it, and as a test observable of the plan-order rule it is defensible. Either name it as that in its field comment, or have a consumer, such as the dispatch note, read it.", "files": ["plugin/lib/plan_status.py"], "oid": "O-9"}, {"goal": "The System Can Be Understood", "title": "`evidence list --json` gives no signal when nothing matches", "recommendation": "Without --json, an unmatched --id prints 'no matching facts'. With --json it prints nothing and exits 0. The report-bug intake reads `evidence list --id <fact_id> --json`, so a mistyped or not-yet-synced fact id yields an empty Reproduction field with no cue. Zero JSON lines is a valid answer, so this is advisory: a stderr line on an empty --json result would be enough.", "files": ["plugin/lib/evidence.py"], "oid": "O-10"}, {"goal": "Records Pass", "title": "The change record's title is stronger than the rule it ships", "recommendation": "The title 'No chunk certified while an earlier one is open' ships to the changelog, but words (--chunks, a scoped subject, a span) still certify out of order by design. The body and the Consumer note state this correctly, so no reader is misled into action, and it clears no bar. A truer headline would name the path rule: 'a path no longer certifies a chunk while an earlier one is open'.", "files": [".prawduct/changes/chunk-plan-order.md"], "oid": "O-11"}], "duration_seconds": 900, "scope": "chunk-plan-order", "scope_chosen_by": "binding", "scope_unresolved_cause": null, "scope_reason": null, "base_extended_from": null, "chunks": ["07"], "chunks_proposed": ["07"], "base_reviewed": "4b37f25fdfc11ccb79eca8fb5500cb8146252852", "record_lint": {"records": [".prawduct/artifacts/api-contract.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-chunk-plan-order.md", ".prawduct/artifacts/data-model.md", ".prawduct/changes/chunk-plan-order.md", ".prawduct/changes/delegate-ceiling-whole-tree-guards.md", ".prawduct/owner-rulings.md", "plugin/methodology/building.md", "plugin/methodology/delegation.md", "plugin/methodology/planning.md", "plugin/skills/critic/SKILL.md", "plugin/skills/critic/review-cycle.md", "plugin/skills/report-bug/SKILL.md"], "chunk_graded": "07", "chunks_graded": ["07"], "plan_graded": ".prawduct/artifacts/build-plan-chunk-plan-order.md", "findings": [], "unchecked": [], "counts": {"chunk-ref-missing": 0, "governed-by-gap": 0, "suite-total-claim": 0, "learnings-over-budget": 0